# Settings for the nightly run (scripts/chain/nightly.mjs), its morning
# report (scripts/chain/report.mjs) and the chain they start
# (scripts/chain/run.sh reads this same file: every CHAIN_* key of
# scripts/chain/chain.env.example works here too). scripts/chain/install-nightly.sh
# writes it from this example with CHAIN_ROOT filled in. KEY=VALUE, no
# expansion; the process environment wins over this file.

# ── the run's own place ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Its directory: the checkout (src/), the runs (runs/), the caches, the state
# (nightly/: tonight.json, history.jsonl, state.json) and the installed scripts
# (bin/).
CHAIN_ROOT=/var/lib/filex-nightly
# ⚠ The lock every build on this host takes - the release chain's, NOT one of
# the nightly run's own: the two must wait for each other, not share the
# memory. Required (`none` runs without one).
CHAIN_LOCK=/var/lib/filex-chain/lock
# Its containers, networks and Document Server subnet, apart from the release
# chain's (a network a stopped run left behind would otherwise collide).
CHAIN_PREFIX=fxnightly
CHAIN_DS_SUBNET=172.30.82.0/24
# What else the host runs, out of the night's way (chain.env.example says how
# run.sh runs these): the night's chain pauses it once the lock is held and
# resumes it when the chain ends, before the nightly build.
# CHAIN_PAUSE_CMD=/usr/local/lib/chain-hooks/emulator-pause.sh
# CHAIN_RESUME_CMD=/usr/local/lib/chain-hooks/emulator-resume.sh
# The caches can be the release chain's: the lock keeps the two runs apart.
# CHAIN_GOMODCACHE=/var/lib/filex-chain/cache/gomod
# CHAIN_PNPM_STORE=/var/lib/filex-chain/cache/pnpm-store
# CHAIN_CYPRESS_CACHE=/var/lib/filex-chain/cache/cypress

# ── what it runs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The branch, and the remote of the checkout in $CHAIN_ROOT/src.
NIGHTLY_BRANCH=main
# NIGHTLY_REMOTE_NAME=origin
# The nightly profile's extras: ds-go,s3-live,shots,realenv (all, the
# default) or none.
# CHAIN_NIGHTLY_EXTRAS=ds-go,s3-live,shots,realenv
# realenv once every so many days (1: every night, 0: never).
# NIGHTLY_REALENV_EVERY_DAYS=7
# realenv's stages (empty: every stage), its networks' first three octets, and
# whether a stage it could not run (an image it could not pull) may pass.
# CHAIN_REALENV_STAGES=tls sso office
# CHAIN_REALENV_PRIV_NET=172.30.52
# CHAIN_REALENV_PUB_NET=203.0.113
# CHAIN_REALENV_ALLOW_SKIP=0
# The app builds and language packs ds-go, the browser round and the shots
# job's app scenes install (see chain.env.example). The packs must cover the
# tree under test whole, or langpack.mjs fails the shots job (the others are
# still taken): the nightly translation's pack checkouts
# (scripts/chain/install-langpacks.sh) are the closest, translated after the
# last night's run, against its tree.
# CHAIN_CONVERT_APP_DIR=/path/to/filex-convert/dist
# CHAIN_SIGN_APP_DIR=/path/to/filex-sign/dist
# CHAIN_LANG_ES_DIR=/path/to/filex-lang-es
# CHAIN_LANG_DE_DIR=/path/to/filex-lang-de
# CHAIN_LANG_FR_DIR=/path/to/filex-lang-fr
# CHAIN_LANG_AR_DIR=/path/to/filex-lang-ar

# s3-live: the Go tests against a REAL S3 provider (scripts/chain/job/s3-live.sh,
# backend/internal/testutil/lives3) - a bucket of their own, which they write to
# and delete from, and a key that reaches that bucket only (the build host's: a
# B2 demo account). They run only on a night the S3 code changed since their
# last green run (nightly-lib S3_LIVE_PATHS); without these settings then, the
# morning report warns instead and names `nightly.mjs s3-live --s3-env FILE`,
# which runs them by hand with credentials brought for one run. The values go
# to the job in a 0600 file of the run and are never logged; keep this file
# 0600 too. The region is part of the request signature (lesson #927): the
# provider's own, never empty. PATH_STYLE=0 asks for virtual-hosted addressing.
# FILEX_TEST_S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.eu-central-003.backblazeb2.com
# FILEX_TEST_S3_REGION=eu-central-003
# FILEX_TEST_S3_BUCKET=
# FILEX_TEST_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
# FILEX_TEST_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# FILEX_TEST_S3_PATH_STYLE=1

# ── when it may run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Windows in which no nightly run may be going, comma-separated
# "<day> HH:MM-HH:MM" in UTC (day Mon..Sun, or * for every day). A run that
# would not be over before the next window starts does not start, and one still
# going NIGHTLY_QUIET_MARGIN_MIN minutes before it is stopped. Put another
# job's window here when it shares the host, for example a weekly
# service-update round starting Mondays 02:00 UTC with a four-hour limit:
NIGHTLY_QUIET=Mon 01:45-06:00
# NIGHTLY_QUIET_MARGIN_MIN=15
# Minutes a run takes when no earlier night says (the last finished one does).
# NIGHTLY_EXPECT_MIN=180
# At most this long waiting for CHAIN_LOCK (less when a window is closer).
# NIGHTLY_LOCK_WAIT_MIN=120
# The time zone the report names nights and hours in.
NIGHTLY_TZ=Europe/Istanbul
# Nights of runs kept under $CHAIN_ROOT/runs, nights kept in history.jsonl.
# NIGHTLY_KEEP_RUNS=14
# NIGHTLY_KEEP_HISTORY=120

# ── the nightly build ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# on: after a green night on a commit not built yet, the image of its public
# form (scripts/chain/nightly-build.sh); off: never.
# NIGHTLY_BUILD=on
# auto: pushed right after the build - every green change, the first one
# included (the owner's decision of 2026-10-06, task #175); manual: built and
# tagged here, pushed by `nightly.mjs publish` (the code's default, for a
# checkout that names no registry); off. A push that fails is in the morning
# report as a warning, and `nightly.mjs publish` pushes that build again.
NIGHTLY_PUBLISH=auto
# The local image (tagged <commit>), the recipe, the public repository the
# export is made in, and how many local images are kept.
# NIGHTLY_IMAGE=filex-nightly
# NIGHTLY_DOCKERFILE=docker/Dockerfile
# NIGHTLY_PUBLIC_REMOTE=https://github.com/BRF-Tech/filex.git
# NIGHTLY_KEEP_IMAGES=3
# Where the build goes, as :nightly and :nightly-<YYYYMMDD>.
NIGHTLY_PUSH_REPO=ghcr.io/brf-tech/filex
# The credential, both or neither (neither: the host's Docker config): the
# account and a file holding its token, one that may write packages (0600).
# With both it logs in with a Docker config of its own and removes it after.
# NIGHTLY_REGISTRY_USER=
# NIGHTLY_REGISTRY_TOKEN_FILE=/var/lib/filex-nightly/registry.token

# ── the desktop side of a nightly build (#266) ──────────────────────────────
# Both off in the code; the build host's settings switch them on. They run
# only on a night that made a nightly build (a green change), after it, from
# the same public form (scripts/chain/nightly-desktop.sh).
#
# edge: the amd64 snap, packed on this host by snapcraft in LXD (core24) and
# uploaded to the Snap Store's edge channel - every night that makes a build.
# The arm64 snap is GitHub's arm64 runner's (NIGHTLY_DESKTOP below). off: no
# amd64 snap.
NIGHTLY_SNAP=edge
# The output of `snapcraft export-login --snaps=filex-app --channels=edge
# --acls=package_access,package_push,package_update,package_release`, in a file
# of its own (0600), read for the upload and never logged.
# NIGHTLY_SNAP_CREDENTIALS_FILE=/var/lib/filex-nightly/snap.credentials
# NIGHTLY_SNAP_NAME=filex-app
# NIGHTLY_SNAPCRAFT=snapcraft
# github: every night that makes a build, the public form of its commit goes
# to ghcr as a source archive (NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_SRC_REPO, signed in as the
# image push is), and GitHub's runners make every desktop package from it -
# Windows x64 and arm64, macOS, Linux x64 and arm64 - and the arm64 snap for
# edge (.github/workflows/nightly-desktop.yml, within the hour), into
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_REPO as :nightly-<date> and :nightly. The morning report
# follows them up and, built, puts them on filex.sh/nightly (NIGHTLY_SITE_CMD).
# off: none.
NIGHTLY_DESKTOP=github
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_SRC_REPO=ghcr.io/brf-tech/filex-nightly-src
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_REPO=ghcr.io/brf-tech/filex-desktop-nightly
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_WORKFLOW_REPO=BRF-Tech/filex
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_WORKFLOW=nightly-desktop.yml
# The ORAS client that pushes and reads those artifacts, as a container, pinned.
# NIGHTLY_ORAS_IMAGE=ghcr.io/oras-project/oras:v1.2.2
# The command that publishes the page and the packages, given the directory
# the page was made in (sh -c '<command> "$1"'); NIGHTLY_SITE_REF names the
# artifact. The repository's own: scripts/chain/nightly-site.sh, installed
# beside this run (the site host pulls the packages from ghcr itself):
# NIGHTLY_SITE_CMD=bash /var/lib/filex-nightly/bin/nightly-site.sh --ssh <site host> --path <site directory>
# Empty: the page is made and kept in NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_DIR/site, and the report
# says it was not published.
# NIGHTLY_SITE_CMD=
# The container the amd64 snap's app is made in (Node 22), pinned.
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_IMAGE=electronuserland/builder:22-wine-05.26
# Where the desktop runs are kept (<date>-<commit>/files, SHA256SUMS, logs;
# site/, the last page), how many, and the clone of the public repository the
# desktop side exports into (one of its own: the image's is NIGHTLY_PUBLIC_DIR).
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_DIR=/var/lib/filex-nightly/desktop
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_KEEP=4
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_PUBLIC_DIR=/var/lib/filex-nightly/public-desktop
# Minutes the desktop side takes here, for the quiet windows.
# NIGHTLY_DESKTOP_EXPECT_MIN=30
# The Go toolchain the packages' CLI is built with, and the caches - the
# release chain's, like the night's own (CHAIN_GOROOT is copied from the golang
# image of backend/go.mod when it is not there yet).
# CHAIN_GOROOT=/var/lib/filex-chain/cache/go
# CHAIN_ELECTRON_CACHE=/var/lib/filex-chain/cache/electron

# ── the morning report ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One JSON POST (group, source, severity, title, message) with a Bearer key
# read from CHAIN_NOTIFY_KEY_FILE (never logged). The chain's own end-of-run
# post is off in a nightly run: this report is the night's one notification.
# CHAIN_NOTIFY_URL=https://notify.example.com/api/notify/v1/send
# CHAIN_NOTIFY_KEY_FILE=/var/lib/filex-nightly/notify.key
# NIGHTLY_NOTIFY_GROUP=infra
# NIGHTLY_NOTIFY_SOURCE=filex-nightly
# Minutes the report waits for a run still going; hours after which a night's
# record is not tonight's any more.
# NIGHTLY_REPORT_WAIT_MIN=120
# NIGHTLY_REPORT_MAX_AGE_H=20
